<TopicPage slug="subjects/visual-arts/intermediate/perspective-and-depth">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Perspective is <strong>the mathematics of the window</strong> — a geometric system for depth
    that conquered Western art in one generation. Learning it is learning a convention so
    powerful it feels like vision itself; mastering it means knowing when to use it and when to
    decline.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the pre-geometric cues, the mirror trick, the constructions, the convention" widget="equationStep" config={perspCfg} />

  <h2>Working methods</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Set the horizon first:</strong> eye level decides everything above and below it (we look down on floors, up at ceilings) — the most common beginner confusion resolves with one drawn line.</li>
    <li><strong>Ellipses are circles in perspective:</strong> the top of a mug is an ellipse whose minor axis points to the vanishing point — drawing it correctly sells the whole surface.</li>
    <li><strong>Measuring by eye:</strong> the pencil-at-arm\'s-length technique (compare widths against heights) — sight-size and comparative measurement are the atelier\'s calibration tools.</li>
    <li><strong>Then break it deliberately:</strong> Cézanne tilted tables, cubism exploded the window (the modernism lesson) — artists who knew the rules one generation dismantled them the next; that sequence (learn, then choose) is the whole ladder\'s pattern.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Panofsky, Perspective as Symbolic Form (1927); the art-history and architecture lessons.</p>
</TopicPage>
